|~ `::vec` and what is declared under it: the refusal of a
   payload of another kind, and the verbs of vectors. A
   verb of vectors serves a vector under any tag, a quote and a doc
   among them, and a set where the set has no verb of its own; a verb
   whose answer names the vector answers under the tags of the subject,
   `concat` under those it shares with the vectors it joins, and `map`,
   whose answer is any value, under none. The inputs of a verb
   are read and held to their invariants before the verb runs.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:vec
  |~~ A vector is an ordered list of values of any kinds, `[1 "a" [2]]`:
      the value of a JSON array and the shape most answers take. Its
      elements are read by their index from zero, `get /0`, a negative
      index from the end, `get /-1`; an index past the end is a failure
      there, and `query` reads softly.

      It is written in brackets, its elements set off by spaces,
      `[1 2 3]`, or by commas as JSON writes them, `[1, 2, 3]`, one form
      a literal; and `::vec[1 2]` is `[1 2]`. A set, a quote and a doc
      are vectors under their tags and read as vectors wherever a vector
      is read but as the payload of a kind, which a kind takes by its own
      tag. JSON writes a vector as an array, and vectors rank after
      paths and before sets, element by element.

      Its verbs count and cut, `count`, `first`, `last`, `take`, `drop`,
      turn each element into another, `map ~(…)`, keep some,
      `filter ~(…)`, rank them, `sort`, fold them, `reduce`, and sum
      their numbers, `sum`. A long answer is read in pieces,
      `drop 20 | take 20`, the rest of it the same query with a tail.
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::vec/map) ::link~(::set)
      ::link~(::qlang/values)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts
        |~~ A vector, read as itself. A value of `::vec` already, a set, a
            quote, a doc and a vector under a tag of its own among them,
            stays as it is, `toVec` answering the vector it holds.
            ::link~(::set/toVec) ::link~(::any/payload)
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | reveals :count
    | reveals :shape
    | reveals :first
    | reveals :last
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::vec` reads its payload as the vector it is, and a value of
            no vector is refused: the pairs of a map are a vector by
            `entries`, `{:id 7} | entries`.
        ~~|
    | proves :takesCommas
        |~~ A comma between every two elements writes the same vector. ~~|
        ~("[1, 2]" | parse | answer | eq [1 2])
    | proves :takesCommasBetweenAllOrNone
        |~~ A literal writes a comma between every two elements or between
            none.
        ~~|
        ~("[1, 2 3]" | parse !| tag | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :keepsAValueOfItsKind
        |~~ A value of `::vec` already, a set, a quote and a vector under a
            tag of its own, stays as it is; `toVec` answers the vector it
            holds.
        ~~|
        ~([#[2 1] ~(a | b) ::Box[1]] | map ~(tag ::vec | tag) | eq [::set ::quote ::Box])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::vec{:id 7} !| get [~(tag) ~(found | tag)] | eq [::vec/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::map]);

:vec/count
  |~~ Answers the number of elements the vector holds, `[1 2 3] | count`
      answering `3` and the empty vector `0`. A quote counts its steps,
      a doc its segments, its prose and its quotes, and a set its
      elements, each held once. A map and a string answer `count` by
      verbs of their own, a map counting its entries and a string its
      characters; a number, null or a boolean answers no `count`, and the
      call is refused with the kinds that do.
      ::link~(::vec/empty)
      ::link~(::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many elements the vector holds. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `count` takes no value: whether the vector holds an element is
            `has 1`, and how many elements hold a predicate is
            `filter ~(gt 1) | count`.
        ~~|
    | proves :countsTheElements
        |~~ Three elements count three. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)
    | proves :countsTheStepsOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote counts its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2 | sub 3) | count | eq 3)
    | proves :countsNothingToZero
        |~~ The empty vector counts zero. ~~|
        ~([] | count | eq 0)
    | proves :countsTheSegmentsOfADoc
        |~~ A doc counts its segments, its prose and its quotes. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ::link~(1) b ~~| | count | eq 3)
    | proves :countsTheElementsOfASetOnce
        |~~ A set counts each element once. ~~|
        ~([1 1 2] | get [~(count) ~(toSet | count)] | eq [3 2])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAValue
        |~~ A value given to `count` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea] | count 1 !| tag | eq ::vec/count/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/size
  |~~ The name other languages give `count`: answers the number of
      elements the vector holds, as `count` does, `[1 2 3] | size`
      answering `3`.
      ::link~(::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many elements the vector holds. ~~| ::number
    | proves :answersAsCount
        |~~ `size` answers as `count`. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | get [~(size) ~(count)] | eq [3 3])
    | impl ~(count);

:vec/length
  |~~ The name other languages give `count`: answers the number of
      elements the vector holds, as `count` does, `[1 2 3] | length`
      answering `3`.
      ::link~(::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many elements the vector holds. ~~| ::number
    | proves :answersAsCount
        |~~ `length` answers as `count`. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | get [~(length) ~(count)] | eq [3 3])
    | impl ~(count);

:vec/empty
  |~~ Answers whether the vector holds no element, `[] | empty`
      answering `true`; a vector of null holds an element, as a set of
      null does, so it is not empty. A map answers `empty` by a verb of
      its own; a string answers no `empty`, and text is asked
      `count | eq 0`.
      ::link~(::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the vector holds no element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfTheEmptyVector
        |~~ The empty vector is empty. ~~|
        ~([] | empty)
    | proves :failsOfAnElement
        |~~ A vector of one element is not empty. ~~|
        ~([1] | empty | not)
    | proves :failsOfANull
        |~~ A vector of null holds an element. ~~|
        ~([null] | empty | not)
    | proves :failsOfASetOfNull
        |~~ A set of null holds an element. ~~|
        ~(#[null] | empty | not)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/first
  |~~ Answers the first element, `[10 20 30] | first` answering `10`, or
      null for the empty vector, so a null answer may be an element that
      is null or no element at all, which `count` tells apart. A quote
      answers its first step, a doc its first segment and a set its least
      element, the first of its one order, `#[:tea :cake :jam] | first`
      answering `:cake`. A map answers its first value by a `first` of its
      own; a string answers no `first`, and its first character is
      `take 1`.
      ::link~(::vec/last) ::link~(::any/query)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The first element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `first` takes no value: the first two elements are `take 2`. ~~|
    | proves :answersTheFirst
        |~~ The first element answers. ~~|
        ~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)
    | proves :answersNullOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector has no first element. ~~|
        ~([] | first | eq null)
    | proves :leavesCountToTellNullApart
        |~~ A null element and no element both answer null, and `count`
            tells them apart.
        ~~|
        ~([[null 1] []] | map ~(get [~(first) ~(count)]) | eq [[null 2] [null 0]])
    | proves :answersTheFirstStepOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote answers its first step. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | first | tag | eq ::call)
    | proves :answersTheLeastElementOfASet
        |~~ A vector answers its first written, its set the least. ~~|
        ~([:tea :cake :jam] | get [~(first) ~(toSet | first)] | eq [:tea :cake])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesACount
        |~~ A count given to `first` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | first 2 !| tag | eq ::vec/first/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/last
  |~~ Answers the last element, `[10 20 30] | last` answering `30`, or
      null for the empty vector, as `first` answers from the other end.
      A quote answers its last step, a doc its last segment and a set its
      greatest element. A map answers its last value by a `last` of its
      own, and a string answers no `last`.
      ::link~(::vec/first)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The last element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `last` takes no value: the last two elements are
            `drop (count | sub 2)`, the count computed against the vector.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheLast
        |~~ The last element answers. ~~|
        ~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)
    | proves :answersNullOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector has no last element. ~~|
        ~([] | last | eq null)
    | proves :answersTheLastStepOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote answers its last step. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | last | get /name | eq :mul)
    | proves :answersTheGreatestElementOfASet
        |~~ A vector answers its last written, its set the greatest. ~~|
        ~([:tea :cake :jam] | get [~(last) ~(toSet | last)] | eq [:jam :tea])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesACount
        |~~ A count given to `last` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | last 2 !| tag | eq ::vec/last/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/sum
  |~~ Answers the sum of the elements, each a number, `[1 2 3 4] | sum`
      answering `10`, and `0` for the empty vector. An element that is
      no number, a string, a record or null among them, is refused at its
      index, so a field of each record is read first, `map /amount | sum`.
      A set adds each element once, its index counting its one order, so
      the set of a vector's elements may sum to less than the vector, and
      a map sums its values by a `sum` of its own. Two fields
      of one record add by `sumOf /price /tax`, and a number is added to each
      element by `map ~(add 1)`.
      ::link~(::vec/avg) ::link~(::any/sumOf)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The sum of the elements, under the tags they share. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `sum` adds numbers; the path of its frame names the first
            element that is none, and a field is read first,
            `map /amount | sum`.
        ~~|
    | proves :addsTheElements
        |~~ The sum adds every element. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)
    | proves :sumsNothingToZero
        |~~ The empty vector sums to zero. ~~|
        ~([] | sum | eq 0)
    | proves :sumsAFieldReadFirst
        |~~ A field of each record is read before the sum. ~~|
        ~([{:amount 900} {:amount 300}] | map /amount | sum | eq 1200)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([1 "two" 3] | sum !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /1 | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/sum/ElementKindNotTakenError ::string /1 #[::number]])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesARecord
        |~~ A record whose field was not read is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([{:amount 900} {:amount 300}] | sum !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /0 | tag) ~(trace | last | path)]
          |  eq [::vec/sum/ElementKindNotTakenError ::map /0])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesNull
        |~~ Null is no number to add. ~~|
        ~([1 null] | sum !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/sum/ElementKindNotTakenError /1])
    | proves :addsOnceWhatASetHolds
        |~~ The set of a vector's elements adds a repeated one once. ~~|
        ~([1 1 2] | get [~(sum) ~(toSet | sum)] | eq [4 3])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/namesTheIndexInTheOneOrderOfASet
        |~~ The index a set is refused at counts its one order, not where
            the element was written.
        ~~|
        ~(["two" 1] | toSet | sum !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/sum/ElementKindNotTakenError /1])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/avg
  |~~ Answers the mean of the elements, each a number, `[1 2 3 4] | avg`
      answering `2.5`, or null for the empty vector, which has no mean:
      the sum of the numbers over their count where the sum stays among the
      finite doubles, `[0 1 6 1] | avg` answering `2` and
      `[1e308 -1e308] | avg` answering `0`, and the running mean where it
      passes them, which stays among them, `[1e308 1e308] | avg` answering
      `1e+308`.
      An element that is no number is refused at its index, as `sum`
      refuses it, so a field of each record is read first,
      `map /age | avg`, and the mean of each group of a `groupBy` comes
      under its key. A set takes the mean of its elements, each once, and
      a map of its values by an `avg` of its own.
      ::link~(::vec/sum) ::link~(::vec/groupBy)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The mean of the elements, under the tags they share, null for
            an empty vector.
        ~~|
        #[::number ::null]
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `avg` takes the mean of numbers; the path of its frame names
            the first element that is none, and a field is read first,
            `map /amount | avg`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheMean
        |~~ The mean of one to four is two and a half. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4] | avg | eq 2.5)
    | proves :averagesZeroOneAndFour
        |~~ The mean of zero, one and four is their sum over three. ~~|
        ~([0 1 4] | avg | eq ([0 1 4] | sum | div 3))
    | proves :answersTheSumOverTheCount
        |~~ The mean of zero, one, six and one is their sum over their count,
            two.
        ~~|
        ~([0 1 6 1] | avg | eq 2)
    | proves :answersNullOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector has no mean. ~~|
        ~([] | avg | eq null)
    | proves :averagesEachGroup
        |~~ The mean of each group comes under its key. ~~|
        ~([{:dept :ops :age 30} {:dept :ops :age 40} {:dept :dev :age 25}] | groupBy /dept | map ~(map /age | avg)
          | eq {:ops 35 :dev 25})
    | proves :averagesTheOppositeEnds
        |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles and its
            negation is zero.
        ~~|
        ~([1e308 -1e308] | avg | eq 0)
    | proves :dividesPastTheEndsByTheCount
        |~~ The mean of three numbers near one end and one near the other
            is half of one of them.
        ~~|
        ~([1e308 1e308 1e308 -1e308] | avg | eq 5e307)
    | proves :averagesOneEnd
        |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles taken twice
            is that number.
        ~~|
        ~([1e308 1e308] | avg | eq 1e308)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([1 "two" 3] | avg !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /1 | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/avg/ElementKindNotTakenError ::string /1 #[::number]])
    | proves :averagesEachElementOfASetOnce
        |~~ The set of a vector's elements counts a repeated one once. ~~|
        ~([1 1 4] | get [~(avg) ~(toSet | avg)] | eq [2 2.5])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/min
  |~~ Answers the least element in the one order of values,
      `[3 1 4] | min` answering `1`, or null for the empty vector. It
      relates elements of one kind, as `lt` relates two values, so two of
      two kinds are refused as the pair, a missing value among them, which
      is taken out first, `filter ~(ne null) | min`. With a key the call
      gives, the element whose key answers least answers whole, the first
      of equal ones, `[{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min /age` answering
      `{:age 20}`. The key is code run on each element, a path, `/age`, or
      a quote, `~(get /age | abs)`, read as `sort` reads its key, so
      `desc` turns it; a value computed at the call other than null, which
      gives no key, is refused, and a key that misses its field answers the
      error of the read. A set answers its least element without a key,
      the first of its one order, and a map its least value by a `min` of
      its own.
      ::link~(::vec/max) ::link~(::vec/sort)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The key the call gives, code run on each element, null when it
            gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /age)`, it
            runs on each element, while `(get /age)` is computed once at the
            call, against the vector, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The least element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :answersTheLeast
        |~~ The least element answers. ~~|
        ~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)
    | proves :answersNullOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector has no least element. ~~|
        ~([] | min | eq null)
    | proves :relatesValuesOfOneKind
        |~~ Two elements of two kinds are refused, a missing one taken out
            first.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~([3 null 1] | min !| tag) ~([3 null 1] | filter ~(ne null) | min)] | eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError 1])
    | proves :answersTheElementOfTheLeastKey
        |~~ The element whose key answers least answers whole. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min /age | eq {:age 20})
    | proves :answersTheFirstOfEqualKeys
        |~~ Of elements whose keys are equal the first answers. ~~|
        ~([{:age 20 :name "Ann"} {:age 20 :name "Bob"}] | min /age | get /name | eq "Ann")
    | proves :turnsTheKeyByDesc
        |~~ A key under `desc` answers the greatest. ~~|
        ~([2 1 3] | min ~(desc) | eq 3)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfTheKey
        |~~ A key that misses its field answers the error of the read. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:name "Bob"}] | min /age !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAStepWithoutItsQuote
        |~~ A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at
            the call, and its error is refused.
        ~~|
        ~([3 1] | min (get /age)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(key | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/min/KeyKindNotTakenError ::path/IndexNotIntegerError])
    | proves :answersTheFirstOfASet
        |~~ A set answers its least element without a key, its first. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 4] | get [~(min) ~(first)] | eq [1 1])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/max
  |~~ Answers the greatest element in the one order of values,
      `[3 1 4] | max` answering `4`, or null for the empty vector, as
      `min` answers from the other end, of elements of one kind, and
      with a key the element whose key answers greatest answers whole, the
      first of equal ones, `[{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max /age` answering
      `{:age 40}`. The key is read as `min` reads it; a set answers its
      greatest element without a key, and a map its greatest value by a
      `max` of its own.
      ::link~(::vec/min)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The key the call gives, code run on each element, null when it
            gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null, `max /age`, as for `min`. ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The greatest element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :answersTheGreatest
        |~~ The greatest element answers. ~~|
        ~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)
    | proves :answersNullOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector has no greatest element. ~~|
        ~([] | max | eq null)
    | proves :relatesValuesOfOneKind
        |~~ A number and a string are no pair a maximum relates. ~~|
        ~([3 "a"] | max !| tag | eq ::call/KindsMismatchedError)
    | proves :answersTheElementOfTheGreatestKey
        |~~ The element whose key answers most answers whole. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max /age | eq {:age 40})
    | proves :answersTheFirstOfEqualKeys
        |~~ Of elements whose keys are equal the first answers. ~~|
        ~([{:age 40 :name "Ann"} {:age 40 :name "Bob"}] | max /age | get /name | eq "Ann")
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A key that is no quote is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | max 1 !| tag | eq ::vec/max/KeyKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :answersTheLastOfASet
        |~~ A set answers its greatest element without a key, its last. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 4] | get [~(max) ~(last)] | eq [4 4])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/map
  |~~ Answers the vector of what the code answers for each element, in
      their order, `[1 2 3] | map ~(add 10)` answering `[11 12 13]`. The
      code is a quote run on each element as its subject: a step of one
      word or one path written short, `map /name` or `map ~count`,
      several steps in one quote, `map ~(mul 2 | add 1)`, and the template
      of `get`, which builds the shape of each element,
      `map ~(get {:id /id :count ~(get /tags | count)})`.
      A quote written in the call reads the names of its step, a name
      declared before the call among them; a quote held in a name runs
      in a lane of its own, which reads the verbs of the tree and no
      value a reader named; and the names a run declares stay in it. The
      first failure the code answers for an element is the answer of the
      call, the elements after it left unrun, and a code that recovers,
      `map ~(add 1 !| 0)`, keeps every element. The answers stand in a
      vector of their own, under no tag of the subject, whose tags speak
      of the elements it held; a quote maps its steps, a doc its segments
      and a set its elements in its one order, equal images each at its
      index, which `toSet` makes a set. A map answers `map` by a verb of
      its own; `filter` keeps the elements a predicate holds of, and
      `reduce` folds them into one value.
      ::link~(::vec/filter) ::link~(::vec/reduce)
      ::link~(::map/map) ::link~(::qlang/code)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code run on each element, a quote. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :CodeKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `map ~(add 1)`, or a path, `map /name`;
            written without the tilde, `map (add 1)`, it is computed once
            at the call, against the vector, and refused with what it
            answered.
        ~~|
    | throws :CodeNotGivenError
        |~~ `map` runs the code the call gives on each element,
            `map ~(add 1)`; a call without it has nothing to run.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ What the code answered for each element, in their order, a
            vector under no tag.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `map` takes one code: several steps run in order inside one
            quote, `map ~(mul 2 | add 1)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersWhatEachElementAnswers
        |~~ Each element is replaced by what the code answers for it. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | map ~(add 10) | eq [11 12 13])
    | proves :takesAPathShort
        |~~ A path alone is written short. ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann"} {:name "Bob"}] | map /name | eq ["Ann" "Bob"])
    | proves :runsSeveralStepsInOneQuote
        |~~ Several steps run in one quote on each element. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | map ~(mul 2 | add 1) | eq [3 5 7])
    | proves :readsANameOfTheScope
        |~~ A name of the code around the call is read inside. ~~|
        ~({:rate 10 :prices [1 2]} | with [/rate /prices] ~(prices | map ~(mul rate)) | eq [10 20])
    | proves :answersTheFirstFailure
        |~~ The first error answers the call. ~~|
        ~([1 "a" "b"] | map ~(add 1) !| trace | first | subject | eq "a")
    | proves :recoversAFailedElement
        |~~ A code that recovers keeps every element. ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | map ~(add 1 !| 0) | eq [2 0])
    | proves :answersUnderNoTag
        |~~ The answers stand under no tag of the subject. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1 2] | map ~(add 1) | eq [2 3])
    | proves :mapsTheStepsOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote maps its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | map /name | eq [:add :mul])
    | proves :answersTheEmptyVector
        |~~ The empty vector answers the empty vector. ~~|
        ~([] | map ~(add 1) | eq [])
    | proves :CodeKindNotTakenError/refusesAStepComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A step written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | map (add 1) !| tag | eq ::vec/map/CodeKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :CodeNotGivenError/refusesACallWithoutCode
        |~~ A call without code is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | map !| tag | eq ::vec/map/CodeNotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoCodes
        |~~ Two codes are refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | map ~(add 1) ~(mul 2) !| tag | eq ::vec/map/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :buildsTheShapeOfEachElementByATemplate
        |~~ A template of `get` builds the shape of each element. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1 :tags [:new :vip]}] | map ~(get {:id /id :count ~(get /tags | count)}) | eq [{:id 1 :count 2}])
    | proves :keepsTheNamesOfARunInside
        |~~ The names a run declares stay in it. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | map ~(:bonus 5; add bonus) | bonus !| tag | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)
    | proves :keepsEveryElementByARecoveringCode
        |~~ A code that recovers keeps every element. ~~|
        ~([1 "a" 3] | map ~(add 1 !| 0) | eq [2 0 4])
    | proves :mapsASetInItsOneOrderIntoAVector
        |~~ A set maps its elements in its one order into a vector, equal
            images each at its index.
        ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 2] | map ~(gt 1) | eq [false true true])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/filter
  |~~ Answers the elements whose predicate answers `true`, in their order,
      `[1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2)` answering `[3 4 5]`. The predicate is
      code run on each element as its subject, a quote,
      `~(get /age | gte 18)`, or a verb by its name, `~empty`; a value
      computed at the call is refused, the error of a read computed at the
      call among them, `filter (get /active)` for `filter /active`. The
      predicate answers a boolean, since no other value stands for one,
      and an error it answers for an element answers the call, so a field
      an element may lack is read softly, `~(query /active | eq true)`. Several
      conditions are one predicate,
      `filter ~(allOf /active (get /age | gte 18))`. The answer keeps the
      kind of the subject: a vector under a tag stays under it, a quote
      keeps the steps that hold, a doc the segments and a set a set, its
      index counting its one order; a map answers `filter` by a verb
      of its own.
      ::link~(::vec/every) ::link~(::any/allOf)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :PredicateKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`, or a path,
            `filter /active`; written without the tilde, `filter (gt 1)`,
            it is computed once at the call and refused.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The elements the predicate accepted, in order. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ConditionKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
            the path of its frame names the element whose predicate
            answered another kind.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `filter` takes one predicate; conditions held together are
            one, `filter ~(allOf /active (get /age | gte 18))`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsWhatThePredicateHolds
        |~~ The elements past two are kept in their order. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])
    | proves :readsEachElement
        |~~ The predicate reads each element as its subject. ~~|
        ~([{:age 25} {:age 15}] | filter ~(get /age | gte 18) | eq [{:age 25}])
    | proves :takesAVerbByItsName
        |~~ A verb by its name stands where a quote would. ~~|
        ~(:number/positive ~(gt 0); [1 -2 3] | filter ~positive | eq [1 3])
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfAnElement
        |~~ An error the predicate answers for an element answers the
            call.
        ~~|
        ~([{:active true} {:name "Bob"}] | filter ~(get /active | eq true) !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :readsAFieldSoftly
        |~~ A field an element may lack is read softly. ~~|
        ~([{:active true} {:name "Bob"}] | filter ~(query /active | eq true) | eq [{:active true}])
    | proves :holdsConditionsTogether
        |~~ Several conditions are one predicate. ~~|
        ~([{:active true :age 20} {:active false :age 30}]
          | filter ~(allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18))
          | eq [{:active true :age 20}])
    | proves :keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A vector under a tag stays under it. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq ::Box[2 3])
    | proves :keepsAQuoteAQuote
        |~~ A quote keeps the steps that hold. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(get /name | eq :mul) | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :keepsASetASetUnderItsTags
        |~~ A set under a tag stays a set under it. ~~|
        ~(::Box#[1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq ::Box#[2 3])
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter (count | gt 1)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(predicate | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/filter/PredicateKindNotTakenError ::boolean])
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesAStepWithoutItsQuote
        |~~ A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at
            the call, and its error is refused.
        ~~|
        ~([{:active true}] | filter (get /active)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(predicate | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/filter/PredicateKindNotTakenError ::path/IndexNotIntegerError])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(predicate | answer | tag) ~(predicate | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/filter/ConditionKindNotTakenError /0 ::number #[::boolean]])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/namesTheIndexInTheOneOrderOfASet
        |~~ The index a set is refused at counts its one order, not where
            the element was written.
        ~~|
        ~(["two" 1] | toSet | filter ~(if (eq 1) ~(true) ~(it))
          !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path)]
          |  eq [::vec/filter/ConditionKindNotTakenError /1])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoPredicates
        |~~ Two predicates are refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | filter ~(gt 1) ~(gt 0) !| tag | eq ::vec/filter/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/every
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers `true` for every element,
      the question a law asks of a listing, `[2 4 6] | every ~(gt 0)`
      answering `true`. It stops at the first element that answers
      `false`, so the elements after it are not run, and the empty
      vector answers `true`. The predicate is code run on each element,
      a quote or a verb by its name, as `filter` takes it: a value
      computed at the call is refused, the predicate answers a boolean,
      and an error it answers for an element answers the call. A set is
      asked in its one order, and a map of its values by an `every` of its
      own; `any` asks whether some element holds.
      ::link~(::vec/any) ::link~(::vec/filter)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :PredicateKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `every ~(gt 0)`, or a path,
            `every /active`; written without the tilde, `every (gt 0)`, it
            is computed once at the call and refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the predicate held for every element, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
            the path of its frame names the element whose predicate
            answered another kind.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsOfEveryElement
        |~~ Every element is positive. ~~|
        ~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0))
    | proves :failsOnOneElement
        |~~ One element below the bar fails it. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | every ~(gt 2) | not)
    | proves :stopsAtTheFirstFalse
        |~~ The elements after the first false are not run. ~~|
        ~([0 "x"] | every ~(gt 0) | not)
    | proves :holdsOfNothing
        |~~ An empty container answers true. ~~|
        ~([] | every ~(gt 0))
    | proves :takesAVerbByItsName
        |~~ A verb by its name stands where a quote would. ~~|
        ~(:number/positive ~(gt 0); [2 4 6] | every ~positive)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfAnElement
        |~~ An error the predicate answers for an element answers the
            call.
        ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:name "Bob"}] | every ~(get /age | gt 0) !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | every (count | gt 1)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(predicate | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/every/PredicateKindNotTakenError ::boolean])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | every ~(1) !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(predicate | answer | tag) ~(predicate | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/every/ConditionKindNotTakenError /0 ::number #[::boolean]])
    | proves :asksASetInItsOneOrder
        |~~ A set asks its number before its string, and the number that
            fails decides, where the vector asks the string first.
        ~~|
        ~(["x" 0] | get [~(every ~(gt 0) !| tag) ~(toSet | every ~(gt 0))]
          | eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError false])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/any
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers `true` for some element,
      `[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2)` answering `true`. It stops at the first
      element that answers `true`, so the elements after it are not run,
      and the empty vector answers `false`. The predicate is code run on
      each element, a quote or a verb by its name, as `filter` takes it:
      a value computed at the call is refused, and the predicate answers
      a boolean. A set is asked in its one order, and a map of its values
      by an `any` of its own; `every` asks whether each element holds.
      ::link~(::vec/every) ::link~(::vec/filter)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :PredicateKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `any ~(gt 2)`, or a path,
            `any /vip`; written without the tilde, `any (gt 2)`, it is
            computed once at the call and refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the predicate held for some element, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
            the path of its frame names the element whose predicate
            answered another kind.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsOfSomeElement
        |~~ One element past two is enough. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2))
    | proves :failsOfNone
        |~~ It is false when no element holds. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 99) | not)
    | proves :stopsAtTheFirstTrue
        |~~ The elements after the first true are not run. ~~|
        ~([3 "x"] | any ~(gt 2))
    | proves :failsOfNothing
        |~~ An empty container answers false. ~~|
        ~([] | any ~(gt 0) | not)
    | proves :takesAVerbByItsName
        |~~ A verb by its name stands where a quote would. ~~|
        ~(:number/big ~(gt 2); [1 2 3] | any ~big)
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | any (count | gt 1)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(predicate | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/any/PredicateKindNotTakenError ::boolean])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | any ~(1) !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(predicate | answer | tag) ~(predicate | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/any/ConditionKindNotTakenError /0 ::number #[::boolean]])
    | proves :asksASetInItsOneOrder
        |~~ A set asks its number before its string, and the number that
            holds decides, where the vector asks the string first.
        ~~|
        ~(["x" 3] | get [~(any ~(gt 2) !| tag) ~(toSet | any ~(gt 2))]
          | eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError true])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/has
  |~~ Answers whether the vector holds an element equal to the element the
      call gives, whether a JSON array holds a value,
      `["ops" "db"] | has "ops"` answering `true`. Elements are compared
      as `eq` compares them: a vector element is found by its content, a
      number whatever its digits, and a keyword is not its string, so
      `[:ops] | has "ops"` answers `false`. A set is searched by its one
      order, `#[:dev :ops :qa] | has :ops` answering `true`, and a map answers
      `has` of its keys by a verb of its own. A vector given is one
      element: whether a set holds each of several is `inter`. A path
      given reads the part it names, `[[1] 2] | has /0/0` answering
      `true`, so a path held as an element is asked for by `contains`,
      which asks for an element alone.
      ::link~(::vec/contains) ::link~(::any/eq)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :element
        |~~ The element looked for, any value, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :ElementNotGivenError
        |~~ `has` asks for the element the call gives, `has 1`; whether the
            vector holds any element is `empty | not`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the vector holds the element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `has` asks for one element: whether a set holds each of several
            is `inter #[1 2] | eq #[1 2]`, and a vector is made a set
            first, `toSet | inter #[1 2] | eq #[1 2]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :findsAnElement
        |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
        ~(["ops" "db"] | has "ops")
    | proves :failsOfAnElementNotHeld
        |~~ An element not held is not found. ~~|
        ~(["ops" "db"] | has "x" | not)
    | proves :findsAnEqualVector
        |~~ A vector element is found by equality. ~~|
        ~([1 [2]] | has [2])
    | proves :findsANumberWhateverItsDigits
        |~~ A number is found whatever its digits. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | has 1.0)
    | proves :keepsAKeywordApartFromItsString
        |~~ A keyword is not its string. ~~|
        ~([:ops] | has "ops" | not)
    | proves :asksWhetherAPathReads
        |~~ A path asks whether it reads, an index within an index among
            them.
        ~~|
        ~([1 [2]] | has /1/0)
    | proves :searchesASetByItsOneOrder
        |~~ A set finds an element it holds and no other, a keyword apart
            from its string.
        ~~|
        ~(#[:qa :dev :ops] | get [~(has :ops) ~(has "ops") ~(has :hr)] | eq [true false false])
    | proves :asksAVectorAsOneElement
        |~~ A vector given is one element, not several. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | has [1 2] | not)
    | proves :ElementNotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoElement
        |~~ A call with no element is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | has !| tag | eq ::vec/has/ElementNotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoElements
        |~~ A call with two elements is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | has 1 2 !| tag | eq ::vec/has/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/contains
  |~~ Answers whether the vector holds an element equal to the element the
      call gives, as `has` asks of an element, under the name a string
      answers for its substrings, `["ops" "db"] | contains "ops"`
      answering `true`; whether a value is one of several reads
      `with {:fruit ~it} ~(["apple" "pear"] | contains fruit)`. A set is searched by its one order,
      and a map answers no `contains` and asks `has` of its keys. A string
      answers `contains` for a piece of its text, where a vector and a set
      hold whole elements alone, so a query that may meet a string or a
      vector asks `contains`.
      ::link~(::vec/has) ::link~(::string/contains)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :element
        |~~ The element looked for, any value, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the vector holds the element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :findsAnElement
        |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
        ~(["ops" "db"] | contains "ops")
    | proves :asksWhetherAValueIsOneOfSeveral
        |~~ A value named first is looked for among several. ~~|
        ~("pear" | with {:fruit ~it} ~(["apple" "pear"] | contains fruit))
    | proves :searchesASetByItsOneOrder
        |~~ A set answers what `has` answers. ~~|
        ~(#[:dev :ops] | get [~(contains :ops) ~(contains :hr)] | eq [true false])
    | proves :holdsOnlyWholeElements
        |~~ A string holds a piece of its text, a vector and a set whole
            elements alone.
        ~~|
        ~(["abc" ["abc"] #["abc"]] | map ~(contains "b") | eq [true false false])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/groupBy
  |~~ Answers the map of vectors the key files the elements into, each
      under the keyword its key answered,
      `[{:dept :ops} {:dept :dev} {:dept :ops}] | groupBy /dept` answering
      `{:ops [{:dept :ops} {:dept :ops}] :dev [{:dept :dev}]}`. A group
      keeps its elements in their order, and the empty vector answers the
      empty map. A key of a map is a keyword, so a key that answers a
      string, the field of a JSON object, or a number files its element
      under the keyword of its text, `:"1"` for `1`, a boolean or null
      under the keyword of its literal, so a condition partitions,
      `[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1)` answering `{:false [1] :true [2 3]}`, and
      a tag name under the keyword of its name, so `groupBy ~(tag)` files
      by kind, and two values whose text is one, `1` and `"1"`, meet and
      are refused; a key of another kind, a vector or a map, is refused at
      its element with the kinds a key files under.
      Without a key an element files under itself, so
      `groupBy | map ~count` counts the occurrences of each value. The key
      is code run on each element, a quote, `~(get /dept)`; a value computed
      at the call is refused, and a key that misses its field answers the
      error of the read. A set answers `groupBy` by a verb of its own, and
      `indexBy` keeps one element under each key.
      ::link~(::vec/indexBy) ::link~(::vec/avg)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that answers the key each element is filed under,
            null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /kind)`, it
            runs on each element, while `(get /kind)` is computed once at
            the call, against the vector, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered. ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, since
            the keys of a map are keywords, or a value `toKeyword` names
            one for: a string or a number files under the keyword of its
            text, a boolean or null under that of its literal and a tag
            name under that of its name. A key of another kind, a vector,
            a set, a map or a quote, is refused, the path of its frame
            naming the element and `:expected` the kinds a key files
            under.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyTakenError
        |~~ Two values the key answers file under one keyword, `1` and
            `"1"` both under `:"1"`, and a map holds the elements of one
            value under a key: the second is refused, `:key` naming the
            keyword, `:taken` the value that filed under it first and
            the path of its frame the element that met it. A key that
            means both answers one kind,
            `~(get /id | if (tag | eq ::string) ~(it) ~(print))`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :taken |~~ The value of the key that filed under the keyword first. ~~|)
    | proves :filesUnderTheKey
        |~~ The elements file under the key they answer. ~~|
        ~([{:dept :ops :name "Ann"} {:dept :dev :name "Bob"} {:dept :ops :name "Eve"}] | groupBy /dept | get /ops | map /name | eq ["Ann" "Eve"])
    | proves :keepsTheOrderInAGroup
        |~~ A group keeps its elements in their order. ~~|
        ~([{:dept :eng :name "a"} {:dept :sales :name "b"} {:dept :eng :name "c"}]
          | groupBy /dept
          | get /eng | map /name
          | eq ["a" "c"])
    | proves :answersTheEmptyMapOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector files into the empty map. ~~|
        ~([] | groupBy /dept | eq {})
    | proves :filesAStringUnderItsKeyword
        |~~ A string key files under its keyword. ~~|
        ~([{:status "paid"} {:status "open"} {:status "paid"}] | groupBy /status | map ~count | eq {:paid 2 :open 1})
    | proves :filesANumberUnderItsText
        |~~ A number key files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
        ~([{:floor 1} {:floor 2} {:floor 1}] | groupBy /floor | keys | eq #[:"1" :"2"])
    | proves :partitionsByACondition
        |~~ A boolean key files under the keyword of its literal. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1) | eq {:false [1] :true [2 3]})
    | proves :filesNullUnderItsLiteral
        |~~ A null key files under `:null`. ~~|
        ~([{:country "US"} {}] | groupBy ~(query /country) | keys | eq #[:US :null])
    | proves :filesATagUnderItsName
        |~~ A tag name files under the keyword of its name. ~~|
        ~([1 "a" 2] | groupBy ~(tag) | eq {:number [1 2] :string ["a"]})
    | proves :filesOneKindOfOneTextTogether
        |~~ A key that answers one kind files the values of one text
            together.
        ~~|
        ~([{:id 1} {:id "1"}] | groupBy ~(get /id | if (tag | eq ::string) ~(it) ~(print)) | map ~count | eq {:"1" 2})
    | proves :filesUnderItselfWithoutAKey
        |~~ Without a key an element files under itself. ~~|
        ~(["tea" "cake" "tea"] | groupBy | eq {:tea ["tea" "tea"] :cake ["cake"]})
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfTheKey
        |~~ A key that misses its field answers the error of the read. ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann"}] | groupBy /dept !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :KeyTakenError/refusesTwoValuesOfOneText
        |~~ A number and a string of one text meet under one key. ~~|
        ~([1 "1"] | groupBy !| get [~(tag) ~(key | answer) ~(taken) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError :"1" 1 /1])
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword given as the key is computed at the call, and
            refused.
        ~~|
        ~([{:dept :ops}] | groupBy :dept
          !| get [~(tag) ~(key | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyKindNotTakenError ::keyword])
    | proves :KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError/refusesAVectorKey
        |~~ A vector key is refused at its element, with the kinds a key
            files under.
        ~~|
        ~([{:dept :ops} {:dept [:ops]}] | groupBy /dept !| get [~(tag) ~(key | answer | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(key | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError ::vec /1 #[::boolean ::keyword ::null ::number ::string ::tag]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/indexBy
  |~~ Answers the map of the elements, each under the keyword its key
      answered, `[{:id :ann :age 30} {:id :bob :age 25}] | indexBy /id`
      answering `{:ann {:id :ann :age 30} :bob {:id :bob :age 25}}`, so an
      entry is read by its key and then by a field, `/ann/age`. Of
      elements that share a key the last wins. A key files under a keyword
      as `groupBy` files it, a string or a number under the keyword of its
      text, a boolean or null under that of its literal and a tag name
      under that of its name, and two values whose text is one, `1` and
      `"1"`, meet and are refused; a key of another kind is refused at its
      element with the kinds a key files under. Without a key an element
      files under itself, a string under its keyword. The key is code run on
      each element, a quote, `~(get /id)`; a value computed at the call is
      refused. A set files its elements in its one order, the last of
      alike keys staying, and `groupBy` keeps every element of a key.
      ::link~(::vec/groupBy)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that answers the key each element is filed under,
            null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /id)`, it
            runs on each element, while `(get /id)` is computed once at the
            call, against the vector, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ Each element under the keyword its key answered, the last of
            alike ones.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :SubjectNotVecError
        |~~ `indexBy` files the elements of a vector, a set's among them: a
            map's values are read first, `vals | indexBy /id`.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, since
            the keys of a map are keywords, or a value `toKeyword` names
            one for: a string or a number files under the keyword of its
            text, a boolean or null under that of its literal and a tag
            name under that of its name. A key of another kind, a vector,
            a set, a map or a quote, is refused, the path of its frame
            naming the element and `:expected` the kinds a key files
            under.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyTakenError
        |~~ Two values the key answers file under one keyword, `1` and
            `"1"` both under `:"1"`, and a map holds one element of one
            value under a key: the second is refused, `:key` naming the
            keyword, `:taken` the value that filed under it first and
            the path of its frame the element that met it. A key that
            means both answers one kind,
            `~(get /id | if (tag | eq ::string) ~(it) ~(print))`.
        ~~|
        ::vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError
    | proves :filesEachUnderItsKey
        |~~ Each element files under its key. ~~|
        ~([{:id :ann :age 30} {:id :bob :age 25}] | indexBy /id | get /bob/age | eq 25)
    | proves :readsAFieldOfAnEntry
        |~~ An entry is read by its key and then by a field. ~~|
        ~([{:id :ann :name "Ann"} {:id :bob :name "Bob"}] | indexBy /id | get /ann/name | eq "Ann")
    | proves :keepsTheLastOfAKey
        |~~ Of elements that share a key the last wins. ~~|
        ~([{:id :ann :age 30} {:id :ann :age 31}] | indexBy /id | get /ann/age | eq 31)
    | proves :filesANumberUnderItsText
        |~~ A number key files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
        ~([{:id 7 :name "ann"}] | indexBy /id | get /7/name | eq "ann")
    | proves :filesABooleanUnderItsLiteral
        |~~ A boolean key files under the keyword of its literal. ~~|
        ~([{:paid true :id 1} {:paid false :id 2}] | indexBy /paid | get /true/id | eq 1)
    | proves :filesNullUnderItsLiteral
        |~~ A null key files under `:null`. ~~|
        ~([{:id null :name "Ann"}] | indexBy /id | get /null/name | eq "Ann")
    | proves :filesATagUnderItsName
        |~~ A tag name key files under the keyword of its name. ~~|
        ~([1 "a"] | indexBy ~(tag) | eq {:number 1 :string "a"})
    | proves :filesAStringUnderItsKeyword
        |~~ Without a key a string files under its keyword. ~~|
        ~(["tea" "cake"] | indexBy | eq {:tea "tea" :cake "cake"})
    | proves :filesAKeywordUnderItself
        |~~ Without a key a keyword files under itself. ~~|
        ~([:tea :cake] | indexBy | eq {:tea :tea :cake :cake})
    | proves :KeyTakenError/refusesTwoValuesOfOneText
        |~~ A number and a string of one text meet under one key. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1} {:id "1"}] | indexBy /id !| get [~(tag) ~(key | answer) ~(taken) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyTakenError :"1" 1 /1])
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword given as the key is computed at the call, and
            refused.
        ~~|
        ~([{:id :ann}] | indexBy :id
          !| get [~(tag) ~(key | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyKindNotTakenError ::keyword])
    | proves :keepsTheLastOfAlikeKeysInTheOneOrderOfASet
        |~~ Of two elements of a set under one key, the last in the one
            order stays.
        ~~|
        ~(#[{:id :ann :age 31} {:id :ann :age 30}] | indexBy /id | get /ann/age | eq 31)
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number has no elements to file. ~~|
        ~(42 | indexBy !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/indexBy/SubjectNotVecError ::number])
    | proves :KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError/refusesAVectorKey
        |~~ A vector key is refused at its element, with the kinds a key
            files under.
        ~~|
        ~([{:id [7]}] | indexBy /id !| get [~(tag) ~(key | answer | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(key | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError ::vec /0 #[::boolean ::keyword ::null ::number ::string ::tag]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/sort
  |~~ Answers the elements in the one order of values, the order every
      value takes against every other, the way a reader ranks what a query
      found, `[3 1 4 1 5] | sort` answering `[1 1 3 4 5]`. Kinds come
      first, null, boolean, number, string, regex, keyword, tag name,
      path, vector, set, map, quote and doc, then every value under
      another tag by the name of its tag, a desc, an excerpt and an error
      at rest among them; strings order by their characters, an upper
      letter before a lower one.

      With a key the call gives the elements rank by what the key answers
      for each, a vector key index by index, so
      `sort ~(get [~(get /count | desc) ~(get /name)])` reads as
      `ORDER BY count DESC, name`, and `eq null` at the first index puts
      null last; elements the key leaves alike keep the order of the
      subject, and `desc` turns the key, or one index of it, to descend.
      The key is code run on each element, a path, `/age`, or a quote,
      `~(get /name | lower)`; a value computed at the call other than
      null, which gives no key, is refused, one key is
      given, and a key that misses its field answers the error of the
      read, so a field an element may lack is read softly, `~(query /age)`.

      The answer keeps the kind of the subject, a vector under a tag and a
      quote among them; a set sorts into a vector, and a map by its
      values, its keys kept, by verbs of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/min) ::link~(::desc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each element, null when the call gives
            none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /age)`, it
            runs on each element, while `(get /age)` is computed once at the
            call, against the vector, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `sort` takes one key; a key that ranks by several indices is a
            vector, `sort ~(get [/last /first])`.
        ~~|
    | proves :ordersNumbers
        |~~ Numbers ascend, equal ones side by side. ~~|
        ~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])
    | proves :ordersKindsFirst
        |~~ Null comes before numbers, and numbers before strings. ~~|
        ~([3 null "x" 1] | sort | eq [null 1 3 "x"])
    | proves :ordersTagsByName
        |~~ Tag names order by their names. ~~|
        ~([::Crate ::Box] | sort | eq [::Box ::Crate])
    | proves :ordersAnUpperLetterFirst
        |~~ Strings order by their code points, an upper letter
            first.
        ~~|
        ~(["b" "B" "a"] | sort | eq ["B" "a" "b"])
    | proves :ordersByTheKey
        |~~ A key orders by what it answers for each element. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | sort /age | eq [{:age 20} {:age 30}])
    | proves :ranksByTheFirstIndex
        |~~ A vector key ranks by its first index. ~~|
        ~([{:last "Lee" :first "Ann"} {:last "Kim" :first "Bob"}] | sort ~(get [/last /first]) | map /first | eq ["Bob" "Ann"])
    | proves :breaksTiesByTheNextIndex
        |~~ A vector key breaks the ties of its first index by its next. ~~|
        ~([{:score 7 :name "Bob"} {:score 9 :name "Eve"} {:score 9 :name "Ann"}] | sort ~(get [~(get /score | desc) /name]) | map /name
          |  eq ["Ann" "Eve" "Bob"])
    | proves :putsNullLast
        |~~ `eq null` at the first index puts null last. ~~|
        ~([3 null 1] | sort ~(get [~(eq null) ~(it)]) | eq [1 3 null])
    | proves :keepsTheOrderOfAlikeElements
        |~~ Elements the key leaves alike keep the order of the subject. ~~|
        ~([{:score 9 :name "Bob"} {:score 7 :name "Eve"} {:score 9 :name "Ann"}] | sort /score | map /name | eq ["Eve" "Bob" "Ann"])
    | proves :descendsUnderDesc
        |~~ A key under `desc` descends. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | sort ~(get /age | desc) | first | get /age | eq 40)
    | proves :turnsTheKey
        |~~ `desc` turns one index of a vector key alone. ~~|
        ~([{:dept :ops :age 30} {:dept :ops :age 41}] | sort ~(get [/dept ~(get /age | desc)]) | map /age | eq [41 30])
    | proves :readsAFieldSoftly
        |~~ A field an element may lack is read softly. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:name "Bob"}] | sort ~(query /age) | eq [{:name "Bob"} {:age 30}])
    | proves :keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A vector under a tag sorts under it. ~~|
        ~(::Rows[{:age 41} {:age 30}] | sort /age | eq ::Rows[{:age 30} {:age 41}])
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfTheKey
        |~~ A key that misses its field answers the error of the read. ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:name "Bob"}] | sort /age !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :answersTheErrorAtAnIndex
        |~~ A vector key whose read at an index misses its field answers
            the error alike.
        ~~|
        ~([{:age 30} {:name "Bob"}] | sort ~(get [/age]) !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number given as the key is computed at the call, and
            refused.
        ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | sort 1 !| get [~(tag) ~(key | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/sort/KeyKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoKeys
        |~~ Two keys are refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([{:last "Lee" :first "Ann"}] | sort /last /first !| tag | eq ::vec/sort/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/take
  |~~ Answers the first elements, as many as the count the call gives,
      `[1 2 3 4 5] | take 3` answering `[1 2 3]`. The count is a whole
      number: past the length the whole vector answers, and a count of
      zero or below takes nothing; a count that is no number, or a
      fraction, is refused under the input. The answer keeps the kind of
      the subject, a vector under a tag, a quote, whose first steps it
      takes, and a set, which takes its least elements, the first of its
      one order. A map and a string answer `take` by verbs of their own,
      a string taking its first characters. `drop` answers the rest, so
      `drop 20 | take 10` reads a slice.
      ::link~(::vec/drop) ::link~(::any/query)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :count |~~ How many. ~~| /0 ::number
    | throws :CountKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The count is a number, `[1 2 3] | take 2`; text that holds one
            is read by `toNumber` first, `take ("2" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :CountNotIntegerError
        |~~ The count is a whole number, `[1 2 3] | take 2`; a fraction is
            rounded first, `take (1.5 | round)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | eq (floor))
    | throws :CountNotGivenError
        |~~ `take` takes as many of the first elements as its call counts,
            `take 2`, and the first alone is `first`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The first elements, as many as the count. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `take` takes one count: the two elements after the first are
            `drop 1 | take 2`.
        ~~|
    | proves :takesTheFirst
        |~~ Three of five are the first three. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :takesTheWholePastTheLength
        |~~ A count past the length takes the whole vector. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | take 99 | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :takesNothingByANegativeCount
        |~~ A negative count takes nothing. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | take -1 | eq [])
    | proves :takesTheFirstStepsOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote takes its first steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2 | sub 3) | take 2 | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))
    | proves :readsASliceAfterADrop
        |~~ A drop and then a take read a slice. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | take 2 | eq [3 4])
    | proves :CountKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no count. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | take "2" !| get [~(tag) ~(count | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/take/CountKindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :CountNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | take 1.5 !| get [~(tag) ~(count | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/take/CountNotIntegerError ::number])
    | proves :keepsTheTag
        |~~ A vector under a tag keeps its tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1 2 3] | take 2 | eq ::Box[1 2])
    | proves :takesTheLeastOfASet
        |~~ A vector takes the first it was written with, its set the
            least, a set again.
        ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | get [~(take 2) ~(toSet | take 2)] | eq [[3 1] #[1 2]])
    | proves :CountNotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCount
        |~~ A call with no count is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake] | take !| tag | eq ::vec/take/CountNotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoCounts
        |~~ A call with two counts is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake] | take 1 2 !| tag | eq ::vec/take/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/drop
  |~~ Answers the elements after the first ones, as many as the count the
      call gives, `[1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2` answering `[3 4 5]`. The count
      is a whole number: past the length the empty vector answers, and a
      count of zero or below drops nothing; a count that is no number, or
      a fraction, is refused under the input. The answer keeps the kind of
      the subject, as `take` keeps it, a set dropping its least elements,
      and a map and a string answer `drop` by verbs of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/take)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :count |~~ How many. ~~| /0 ::number
    | throws :CountKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The count is a number, `[1 2 3] | drop 2`; text that holds one
            is read by `toNumber` first, `drop ("2" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :CountNotIntegerError
        |~~ The count is a whole number, `[1 2 3] | drop 2`; a fraction is
            rounded first, `drop (1.5 | round)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | eq (floor))
    | returns |~~ The elements after the ones dropped. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `drop` takes one count: the two elements after the first are
            `drop 1 | take 2`.
        ~~|
    | proves :dropsTheFirst
        |~~ Dropping two leaves the last three. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])
    | proves :dropsTheWholePastTheLength
        |~~ A count past the length leaves the empty vector. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | drop 99 | eq [])
    | proves :dropsNothingByANegativeCount
        |~~ A negative count drops nothing. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | drop -1 | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :keepsAQuoteAQuote
        |~~ A quote drops its first steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | drop 1 | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :CountNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | drop 1.5 !| get [~(tag) ~(count | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/drop/CountNotIntegerError ::number])
    | proves :CountKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | drop "1" !| tag | eq ::vec/drop/CountKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :dropsTheLeastOfASet
        |~~ A vector drops the first it was written with, its set the
            least, a set again.
        ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | get [~(drop 2) ~(toSet | drop 2)] | eq [[2] #[3]])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoCounts
        |~~ A call with two counts is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake :jam] | drop 1 2 !| tag | eq ::vec/drop/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/distinct
  |~~ The name other languages give `toSet`: answers the set of the
      elements, as `toSet` does, `[2 1 2 3 1] | distinct` answering
      `#[1 2 3]`.
      ::link~(::vec/toSet)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The elements, each once, in the one order: a set. ~~|
        ::set
    | proves :answersAsToSet
        |~~ `distinct` answers as `toSet`, under a tag of the subject
            too.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box[2 1 2] | get [~(distinct) ~(toSet)] | eq [#[1 2] #[1 2]])
    | impl ~(toSet);

:vec/reverse
  |~~ Answers the elements in the reverse order, `[1 2 3] | reverse`
      answering `[3 2 1]`. The answer keeps the kind of the subject, a
      vector under a tag and a quote, whose steps it reverses; a set and
      a map answer `reverse` by verbs of their own, a set reversing into
      a vector.
      ::link~(::vec/sort)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The elements in reverse order. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :reversesTheOrder
        |~~ The elements come in reverse order. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
    | proves :keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A vector under a tag reverses under it. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1 2 3] | reverse | eq ::Box[3 2 1])
    | proves :reversesTheStepsOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote reverses its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | reverse | eq ~(mul 2 | add 1))
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/flat
  |~~ Answers the elements with one level of nesting spliced in,
      `[[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat` answering `[1 2 3 4 5]`. An element that
      is a vector splices its elements whatever tag it stands under, so a
      set, a quote and a doc splice too, a quote its steps and a doc its
      segments; a vector deeper inside stays whole, and an element that is
      no vector stays as it is, `[1 [2]] | flat` answering `[1 2]`. The
      answer keeps the kind and the tags of the subject,
      `::Box[[1] [2]] | flat` answering `::Box[1 2]`, so a set of sets
      splices into the set of their elements, their union,
      `#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat` answering `#[1 2 3]`; `append` puts a
      vector in as one element, and `flat` after it splices it.
      ::link~(::vec/append) ::link~(::set/union)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotVecError
        |~~ `flat` splices the vectors a vector holds, a set's among them: a
            map's values are read first, `vals | flat`.
        ~~|
    | proves :splicesOneLevel
        |~~ The inner vectors splice into one. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])
    | proves :splicesAVectorUnderATag
        |~~ A set and a vector under a tag splice. ~~|
        ~([#[2 1] ::Box[3]] | flat | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :splicesTheStepsOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote splices its steps. ~~|
        ~([~(add 1) [2]] | flat | first | tag | eq ::call)
    | proves :keepsADeeperVector
        |~~ A vector two levels in stays whole. ~~|
        ~([[1 [2]] 3] | flat | eq [1 [2] 3])
    | proves :keepsAnElementThatIsNoVector
        |~~ An element that is no vector stays as it is. ~~|
        ~([1 [2]] | flat | eq [1 2])
    | proves :keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ The tags of the subject stay over the answer. ~~|
        ~(::Box[[1] [2]] | flat | eq ::Box[1 2])
    | proves :unitesTheSetsASetHolds
        |~~ A set of sets splices into their union, an element two of them
            hold held once.
        ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [2 3]] | get [~(flat) ~(map ~(toSet) | toSet | flat)] | eq [[1 2 2 3] #[1 2 3]])
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A boolean holds nothing to splice. ~~|
        ~(true | flat !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/flat/SubjectNotVecError ::boolean])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/flatten
  |~~ The name other languages give `flat`: answers the elements with one
      level of nesting spliced in, as `flat` does,
      `[[1 2] [3]] | flatten` answering `[1 2 3]`; a vector deeper inside
      stays whole, `[[1 [2]] 3] | flatten` answering `[1 [2] 3]`.
      ::link~(::vec/flat)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotVecError
        |~~ `flatten` splices a vector as `flat` does: a map's values are
            read first, `vals | flatten`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersAsFlat
        |~~ `flatten` answers as `flat`, one level spliced in. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [3 [4]]] | get [~(flatten) ~(flat)] | eq [[1 2 3 [4]] [1 2 3 [4]]])
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number holds nothing to splice. ~~|
        ~(42 | flatten !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/flatten/SubjectNotVecError ::number])
    | impl ~(flat);

:vec/concat
  |~~ Answers the subject with the elements of each vector the call gives
      after its own, in their order, `[1] | concat [2 3] [4]` answering
      `[1 2 3 4]`, so `reduce [] ~(concat)` joins the vectors a vector
      holds. The vectors join beneath the tags they share, a bare one
      sharing any; `append` puts one element at the end.
      ::link~(::vec/flat) ::link~(::vec/append)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :vectors |~~ The vectors joined after the subject. ~~| ~(it) [::vec]
    | throws :VectorsElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ What `concat` joins is a vector, `concat [2 3]`; a single
            element is put at the end by `append`, `append 2`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements of the subject and of each vector, in order,
            under the tags they share.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | proves :joinsTheVectorsAfterTheSubject
        |~~ The vectors join after the subject in their order. ~~|
        ~([1] | concat [2 3] [4] | eq [1 2 3 4])
    | proves :foldsTheVectorsOfAVector
        |~~ A fold by `concat` joins the vectors a vector holds. ~~|
        ~([[1] [2 3]] | reduce [] ~(concat) | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A vector under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1] | concat [2] | eq ::Box[1 2])
    | proves :VectorsElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no vector to join. ~~|
        ~([1] | concat 2 !| tag | eq ::vec/concat/VectorsElementKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/zip
  |~~ Answers the vectors the subject holds read side by side, one vector
      of the elements at each index, as long as the shortest:
      `[["Ann" "Bob"] [30 25]] | zip` answers `[["Ann" 30] ["Bob" 25]]`,
      and the empty vector answers `[]`. Each element is a vector, a set,
      a quote and a doc among them; a single vector is put beside the
      others in a vector first, `get [~(get /names) ~(get /ages)] | zip`,
      and a set answers the vector of its columns by a `zip` of its own.
      It takes no value.
      ::link~(::vec/flat) ::link~(::set/zip)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The elements at each index, a vector each. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `zip` reads vectors side by side; the path of its frame names
            the first element that is no vector, the vectors read into one first,
            `get [/names /ages] | zip`.
        ~~|
    | proves :pairsTheElementsAtEachIndex
        |~~ The elements at each index stand together. ~~|
        ~([["Ann" "Bob"] [30 25]] | zip | eq [["Ann" 30] ["Bob" 25]])
    | proves :stopsAtTheShortest
        |~~ The answer is as long as the shortest vector. ~~|
        ~([["Ann" "Bob" "Eve"] [30]] | zip | eq [["Ann" 30]])
    | proves :answersNothingOfNothing
        |~~ The empty vector answers the empty vector. ~~|
        ~([] | zip | eq [])
    | proves :readsFieldsSideBySide
        |~~ Two fields of a record read side by side pair their elements.
        ~~|
        ~({:names ["ann" "bob"] :ages [30 40]} | get [/names /ages] | zip | eq [["ann" 30] ["bob" 40]])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An element that is no vector is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] 3] | zip !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | found | get /1 | tag) ~(subject | expected)] | eq [::vec/zip/ElementKindNotTakenError /1 ::number #[::vec]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/reduce
  |~~ Answers the seed folded over the elements by the reducer, each
      element folded into the value so far,
      `[1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add)` answering `15`; the empty vector
      answers the seed. The seed comes first and the reducer after it, so
      `reduce ~(add)` reads the quote as the seed and is refused for the
      reducer it lacks. The reducer is code, a path or a quote. A quote
      of one word, `~(add)` or `~add`, or of its address,
      `~(number/add)`, names the verb the fold calls with the value so far
      as its subject and the element as its first arg; any other code runs
      on the pair of the value so far and the element, and what it answers
      is the value so far after that element:
      `[{:qty 2} {:qty 3}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 (get /1 | get /qty))`
      answers `5`. A failure the reducer answers answers the call. Adding
      from zero sums, multiplying from one gives the product, appending
      from the empty string joins strings and uniting from the empty set
      merges sets. A set folds its elements in its one order, not as they
      were written, and a map folds its values by a `reduce` of its own.
      ::link~(::vec/sum)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :seed |~~ The value the fold starts from, any value. ~~| /0
    | takes :reducer
        |~~ Code: a quote of one word, which names the verb the fold calls
            with the element, or a path or a quote it runs on the pair of
            the value so far and the element.
        ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :ReducerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The reducer is a path or a quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`, after the
            seed: written first, `reduce ~(add) 0`, it is read as the
            seed and the seed as the reducer, and written
            without the tilde, `(add)`, it is computed once at the call;
            either is refused.
        ~~|
    | throws :ReducerNotGivenError
        |~~ `reduce` takes the seed first and the reducer after it,
            `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a call of the reducer alone reads it as the
            seed.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The value folded after the last element, any value, the seed
            for an empty vector.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :sums
        |~~ Adding from zero sums. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)
    | proves :multiplies
        |~~ Multiplying from one gives the product. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 1 ~(mul) | eq 120)
    | proves :joinsStrings
        |~~ Appending from the empty string joins. ~~|
        ~(["a" "b" "c"] | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "abc")
    | proves :unitesSets
        |~~ Uniting from the empty set merges. ~~|
        ~([#[1] #[2 3]] | reduce #[] ~(union) | eq #[1 2 3])
    | proves :takesTheQuoteOfAName
        |~~ The quote of a name alone is a reducer. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~add | eq 6)
    | proves :takesTheAddressOfAVerb
        |~~ A verb named by its address is a reducer. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(number/add) | eq 6)
    | proves :runsCodeOnThePair
        |~~ Code of more than one word runs on the pair of the value so
            far and the element.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(get [~(get /0 | mul 10) /1] | sum) | eq 123)
    | proves :foldsAFieldOfEachElement
        |~~ Code reads a field of each element from the pair. ~~|
        ~([{:qty 2} {:qty 3}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 (get /1 | get /qty)) | eq 5)
    | proves :takesAName
        |~~ A name takes the value so far as its subject and the element
            as its first arg.
        ~~|
        ~(:number/larger (::fn | takes :other |~~ The element. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(if (gt other) ~(it) ~(other))); [3 1 4 1 5] | reduce 0 ~larger | eq 5)
    | proves :takesANameThatLeavesTheElement
        |~~ A name may leave the element unread. ~~|
        ~(:number/tally (::fn | takes :el |~~ The element, unread. ~~| /0 | impl ~(add 1)); [10 20 30] | reduce 0 ~tally | eq 3)
    | proves :answersTheSeedOfNothing
        |~~ The seed is the first value so far, answered as it is for an
            empty subject.
        ~~|
        ~([] | reduce 42 ~(add) | eq 42)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfTheReducer
        |~~ An error the reducer answers answers the call. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | reduce 0 ~(add) !| tag | eq ::number/add/AddendKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ReducerKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 1
          !| get [~(tag) ~(reducer | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/reduce/ReducerKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :ReducerKindNotTakenError/refusesTheReducerFirst
        |~~ A reducer written before the seed is read as the seed, and
            the seed as the reducer.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reduce ~(add) 0
          !| get [~(tag) ~(reducer | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/reduce/ReducerKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :ReducerNotGivenError/readsALoneQuoteAsTheSeed
        |~~ A call of the reducer alone lacks the reducer. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | reduce ~(add) !| tag | eq ::vec/reduce/ReducerNotGivenError)
    | proves :takesANameOfTwoSteps
        |~~ Two steps under a name of their own fold as one verb. ~~|
        ~(:number/step (::fn | takes :el |~~ The element. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(mul 2 | add el)); [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(step) | eq 11)
    | proves :foldsASetInItsOneOrder
        |~~ A vector folds as written, its set in the one order. ~~|
        ~(["b" "a"] | get [~(reduce "" ~(append)) ~(toSet | reduce "" ~(append))] | eq ["ba" "ab"])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/union
  |~~ Answers the union of the values the vector holds, from the first:
      sets unite in the one order, `[#[:tea :cake] #[:cake :jam]] | union`
      answering `#[:cake :jam :tea]`, maps merge with the last value
      winning a key, `[{:font 12} {:font 14 :lang :en}] | union` answering
      `{:font 14 :lang :en}`, and a vector of one value answers it. The
      values are sets or maps: one that is neither is refused at its
      index, a set beside a map with the pair, and the vector holds one
      value at least. The call takes no value, and two values unite as
      `#[:tea] | union #[:cake]`; the sets a set holds,
      whose own `union` meets the set its call gives, unite by this verb
      called by its address, `#[#[1] #[2]] | vec/union`, or by `flat`.
      ::link~(::vec/minus) ::link~(::vec/inter)
      ::link~(::set/union)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The union of the values, a set or a map. ~~|
        #[::set ::map]
    | throws :SubjectEmptyError
        |~~ `union` over a vector unites its values from the first,
            `[a b c] | union`, so the vector holds one at least; two
            values unite as `a | union b`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The values `union` unites are sets or maps; the path of its
            frame names the first that is neither, and a vector of vectors unites once
            each is a set, `map ~(toSet) | union`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `union` over a vector unites the values it holds and takes no
            value: two values unite as `a | union b`, `#[1] | union #[2]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :unitesTheValues
        |~~ The sets the vector holds unite. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea :cake] #[:cake :jam]] | union | eq #[:cake :jam :tea])
    | proves :mergesMapsTheLastWinning
        |~~ Maps merge with the last value winning a key. ~~|
        ~([{:font 12} {:font 14 :lang :en}] | union | eq {:font 14 :lang :en})
    | proves :answersAValueAlone
        |~~ A vector of one value answers it. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea]] | union | eq #[:tea])
    | proves :SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
        |~~ The empty vector holds no value. ~~|
        ~([] | union !| tag | eq ::vec/union/SubjectEmptyError)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAtItsIndex
        |~~ A value that is neither a set nor a map is refused at its
            index.
        ~~|
        ~([#[:tea] 2] | union !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | found | get /1 | tag) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/union/ElementKindNotTakenError /1 ::number #[::map ::set]])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/unitesVectorsMadeSets
        |~~ A vector of vectors unites once each is a set. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [2 3]] | map ~(toSet) | union | eq #[1 2 3])
    | proves :refusesASetAndAMap
        |~~ A set and a map do not unite. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea] {:font 12}] | union
          !| get [~(tag) ~(leftTag) ~(rightTag)]
          |  eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError ::set ::map])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAValueGiven
        |~~ A value the call gives is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([#[1]] | union #[2] !| tag | eq ::vec/union/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :unitesTheSetsASetHoldsByItsAddress
        |~~ The sets a set holds unite by the verb called by its address.
        ~~|
        ~(#[#[1] #[2]] | vec/union | eq #[1 2])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/minus
  |~~ Answers the first value the vector holds less each one after it: a
      set loses the elements the next set holds,
      `[#[:tea :cake :jam] #[:cake]] | minus` answering `#[:jam :tea]`, and
      a map loses the keys of the next, which is a map, a set or a vector
      of keys, each a keyword, a number or a string,
      `[{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} [:tmp]] | minus` answering `{:name "Ann"}`; a
      key of another kind is refused at its element. The first value is a
      set or a map, refused at its index when it is neither, a value after
      it the algebra has no rule for is refused with the pair, and the
      vector holds one value at least. The call takes no value, and of two
      values `#[:tea :cake] | minus #[:cake]` takes the second from the
      first; the sets a set holds meet in its one order as the vector it is,
      `toVec | minus`, or by this verb called by its address,
      `#[#[1 2] #[1]] | vec/minus` answering `#[]`.
      ::link~(::vec/union) ::link~(::vec/inter)
      ::link~(::set/minus)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The first value less the ones after it, a set or a map. ~~|
        #[::set ::map]
    | throws :SubjectEmptyError
        |~~ `minus` over a vector takes from its first value the ones
            after it, `[a b c] | minus`, so the vector holds one at least;
            of two values `a | minus b` takes `b` from `a`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The value `minus` takes from is a set or a map; the path of
            its frame names the first value of the vector, which is
            neither.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword, a number or a
            string, as `get` reads a key alone; the path of its frame
            names the first key of a value of the vector that is none of
            them, and `:expected` the three kinds.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `minus` over a vector takes from its first value the ones
            after it and takes no value: of two values `a | minus b` takes
            `b` from `a`, `#[1 2] | minus #[2]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :subtractsTheRest
        |~~ The first set loses what the next holds. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea :cake :jam] #[:cake]] | minus | eq #[:jam :tea])
    | proves :takesKeysOutOfAMap
        |~~ A map loses the keys of a vector after it, a string among
            them.
        ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} ["tmp"]] | minus | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :takesTheKeysOfAMap
        |~~ A map loses the keys of a map after it. ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} {:tmp null}] | minus | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
        |~~ The empty vector holds no value. ~~|
        ~([] | minus !| tag | eq ::vec/minus/SubjectEmptyError)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberFirst
        |~~ A first value that is neither a set nor a map is refused at
            its index.
        ~~|
        ~([1 #[1]] | minus !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)] | eq [::vec/minus/ElementKindNotTakenError /0 #[::map ::set]])
    | proves :refusesAMapAfterASet
        |~~ A map after a set is refused with the pair. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea] {:font 12}] | minus
          !| get [~(tag) ~(leftTag) ~(rightTag)]
          |  eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError ::set ::map])
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A boolean among the keys is refused at its index, with the
            kinds of a key.
        ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann"} [:tmp true]] | minus !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /1/1 | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/minus/KeyKindNotTakenError ::boolean /1/1 #[::keyword ::number ::string]])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAValueGiven
        |~~ A value the call gives is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([#[1]] | minus #[2] !| tag | eq ::vec/minus/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :takesTheSetsASetHoldsInItsOneOrder
        |~~ The sets a set holds meet in its one order, the sets a vector
            holds in the order written.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(#[#[1 2] #[1]] | vec/minus) ~([#[1 2] #[1]] | minus)] | eq [#[] #[2]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/inter
  |~~ Answers what the values the vector holds share: a set keeps the
      elements every set after it holds,
      `[#[:tea :cake :jam] #[:cake :pie]] | inter` answering `#[:cake]`, and
      a map keeps the keys of the next, which is a map, a set or a vector
      of keys, with its own values,
      `[{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} {:name null}] | inter` answering
      `{:name "Ann"}`, each key of a vector of keys a keyword, a number or a
      string and a key of another kind refused at its element. The first
      value is a set or a map, refused at its index when it is neither, a
      value after it the algebra has no rule for is refused with the pair,
      and the vector holds one value at least. The call takes no value, and
      of two values `#[:tea :cake] | inter #[:cake]` keeps what the first
      shares with the second; what the sets a set holds share is
      this verb called by its address, `#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | vec/inter`.
      ::link~(::vec/union) ::link~(::vec/minus)
      ::link~(::set/inter)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ What every value holds, a set or a map. ~~| #[::set ::map]
    | throws :SubjectEmptyError
        |~~ `inter` over a vector keeps what its values share from the
            first, `[a b c] | inter`, so the vector holds one at least; of
            two values `a | inter b` keeps what `a` shares with `b`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The value `inter` keeps from is a set or a map; the path of
            its frame names the first value of the vector, which is
            neither.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword, a number or a
            string, as `get` reads a key alone; the path of its frame
            names the first key of a value of the vector that is none of
            them, and `:expected` the three kinds.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `inter` over a vector keeps what its values share and takes no
            value: of two values `a | inter b` keeps what `a` shares with
            `b`, `#[1 2] | inter #[2]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheCommonElements
        |~~ The elements both sets hold stay. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea :cake :jam] #[:cake :pie]] | inter | eq #[:cake])
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfAMapWithItsValues
        |~~ A map keeps the keys of a map after it, with its own values. ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} {:name null}] | inter | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfASet
        |~~ A map keeps the keys a set after it holds. ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} #[:name]] | inter | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
        |~~ The empty vector holds no value. ~~|
        ~([] | inter !| tag | eq ::vec/inter/SubjectEmptyError)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberFirst
        |~~ A first value that is neither a set nor a map is refused at
            its index.
        ~~|
        ~([1 #[1]] | inter !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)] | eq [::vec/inter/ElementKindNotTakenError /0 #[::map ::set]])
    | proves :refusesAMapAfterASet
        |~~ A map after a set is refused with the pair. ~~|
        ~([#[:tea] {:font 12}] | inter
          !| get [~(tag) ~(leftTag) ~(rightTag)]
          |  eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError ::set ::map])
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector among the keys is refused at its index, with the kinds
            of a key.
        ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann"} [[:name]]] | inter !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /1/0 | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/inter/KeyKindNotTakenError ::vec /1/0 #[::keyword ::number ::string]])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAValueGiven
        |~~ A value the call gives is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([#[1]] | inter #[2] !| tag | eq ::vec/inter/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :keepsWhatTheSetsASetHoldsShareByItsAddress
        |~~ What the sets a set holds share is the verb called by its
            address.
        ~~|
        ~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | vec/inter | eq #[2])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/join
  |~~ Answers the strings and keywords the vector holds joined into one,
      the separator the call gives between every two,
      `["a" "b" "c"] | join ","` answering `"a,b,c"`; the empty vector
      joins to the empty string, and `split` takes the string apart again.
      Every element is a string or a keyword, written as its name, so the
      pairs of a map of strings join as they stand,
      `{:host "x"} | entries | map ~(join "=")` answering `["host=x"]`;
      another value is refused at its index, a number made text first by
      `toString`, `["a" 2] | map ~toString | join "+"` answering
      `"a+2"`, and any value written as its literal by `print`. A set
      joins its elements in the one order; a map, a string or a number is refused under the verb, and a
      map's values are read first, `vals | join ","`.
      ::link~(::string/split) ::link~(::any/print)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :separator
        |~~ The text written between every two elements, `join ", "`. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::string]
    | throws :SeparatorKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The separator is a string, `["a" "b"] | join ","`, or a
            keyword written as its name; a number writes as text by
            `toString` first, `join (1 | toString)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements joined into one string by the separator, under the
            tags its strings share.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | throws :SubjectNotVecError
        |~~ `join` writes a vector of strings as one: a map's values are
            read first, `vals | join ","`, and a string is taken apart by
            `split`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Every element `join` writes is a string or a keyword, written
            as its name; the path of its frame names the first that is
            neither, and
            `map ~toString` makes a number its text first, `map ~print`
            writing any value as its literal.
        ~~|
    | proves :joinsWithTheSeparator
        |~~ The separator stands between the strings. ~~|
        ~(["a" "b" "c"] | join "," | eq "a,b,c")
    | proves :joinsNothingToTheEmptyString
        |~~ The empty vector joins to the empty string. ~~|
        ~([] | join "," | eq "")
    | proves :isUndoneBySplit
        |~~ `split` takes the joined string apart again. ~~|
        ~(["a" "b"] | join "," | split "," | eq ["a" "b"])
    | proves :writesAKeywordAsItsName
        |~~ The pairs of the entries of a map of strings join, each key
            written as its name.
        ~~|
        ~({:host "db" :port "80"} | entries | map ~(join "=") | eq ["host=db" "port=80"])
    | proves :writesAKeywordSeparatorAsItsName
        |~~ A keyword separator is written as its name. ~~|
        ~(["1920" "1080"] | join :x | eq "1920x1080")
    | proves :joinsValuesMadeText
        |~~ Values made text first join. ~~|
        ~(["a" 2] | map ~toString | join "+" | eq "a+2")
    | proves :joinsValuesWrittenAsLiterals
        |~~ Values written as their literals join, a keyword with its
            colon.
        ~~|
        ~([1 :tea] | map ~(print) | join "+" | eq "1+:tea")
    | proves :joinsASetInTheOneOrder
        |~~ A set joins its elements in the one order. ~~|
        ~(#["b" "a"] | join "," | eq "a,b")
    | proves :joinsTheValuesOfAMap
        |~~ The values of a map are read first. ~~|
        ~({:first "Ann" :last "Lee"} | vals | join " " | eq "Ann Lee")
    | proves :SeparatorKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number separator is refused. ~~|
        ~(["a" "b"] | join 42
          !| get [~(tag) ~(separator | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::vec/join/SeparatorKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no vector of strings. ~~|
        ~(42 | join "," !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/join/SubjectNotVecError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map is no vector of strings. ~~|
        ~({:first "Ann"} | join "," !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::vec/join/SubjectNotVecError ::map])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number among the strings is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(["a" 42 "c"] | join "," !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /1 | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::vec/join/ElementKindNotTakenError ::number /1 #[::keyword ::string]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/prepend
  |~~ Answers the vector with the element the call gives at its head,
      any value, `[2 3] | prepend 1` answering `[1 2 3]`; a vector given
      is one element, `[1] | prepend [0]` answering `[[0] 1]`. The answer
      keeps the kind of the subject: a vector under a tag stays under it,
      a quote takes the element as its first step, and a set takes it
      through its constructor, so it stays a set in the one order. A
      string answers `prepend` by a verb of its own; `append` puts the
      element at the end.
      ::link~(::vec/append)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :element |~~ The element put at the head, any value. ~~| /0
    | returns |~~ The vector with the element as its first. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :putsTheElementFirst
        |~~ The element goes first. ~~|
        ~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :putsAVectorInAsOneElement
        |~~ A vector given is one element. ~~|
        ~([1] | prepend [0] | eq [[0] 1])
    | proves :keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A vector under a tag takes the element under it. ~~|
        ~(::Box[2] | prepend 1 | eq ::Box[1 2])
    | proves :keepsASetASet
        |~~ A set takes the element as a set does. ~~|
        ~(#[2 3] | prepend 3 | eq #[2 3])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/append
  |~~ Answers the vector with the element the call gives at its end, any
      value, `[1 2] | append 3` answering `[1 2 3]`; a vector given is one
      element, so `[1] | append [2 3]` answers `[1 [2 3]]`, and `flat`
      after it splices the vector in. The answer keeps the kind of the
      subject: a vector under a tag stays under it, a quote takes the
      element as its last step, a set takes it through its constructor,
      so it stays a set, and a doc takes a segment, a string or a quote,
      and refuses another value. A string answers `append` by a verb of
      its own; `prepend` puts the element at the head.
      ::link~(::vec/prepend) ::link~(::vec/flat)
      ::link~(::doc/ElementKindNotTakenError)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :element |~~ The element put at the end, any value. ~~| /0
    | returns |~~ The vector with the element as its last. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :putsTheElementLast
        |~~ The element goes last. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :putsAVectorInAsOneElement
        |~~ A vector given is one element, which `flat` splices. ~~|
        ~([1] | append [2 3] | get [~(it) ~(flat)] | eq [[1 [2 3]] [1 2 3]])
    | proves :keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A vector under a tag takes the element under it. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1 2] | append 3 | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
    | proves :keepsASetASet
        |~~ A set takes the element as a set does. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | append 1 | eq #[1 2])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/toSet
  |~~ Answers the set of the vector's elements, `[2 1 2] | toSet`
      answering `#[1 2]`: the one order and no duplicates, two elements
      one when `eq` says so, so two maps, vectors or sets of the same
      content collapse and a number is one whatever its digits, and
      `count` after it counts the distinct values. The set is of another
      kind than the vector, so it leaves the tags of the subject behind,
      `::Box[2 1 2] | toSet` answering `#[1 2]`, while `within ~(toSet)`
      keeps them. `distinct` answers as it does, and `toVec` turns the
      set back into a vector. A map's keys are a set already, `keys`.
      ::link~(::vec/distinct) ::link~(::set/toVec)
      ::link~(::map/keys) ::link~(::any/eq)
      ::link~(::tagged/within)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The set of the elements. ~~| ::set
    | proves :leadsAMapToItsKeys
        |~~ A map is led to its keys. ~~|
        ~({:id 7} | toSet !| nearest | eq [:keys])
    | proves :mergesEqualMaps
        |~~ Two maps of the same entries are one. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1} {:id 2} {:id 1}] | toSet | eq #[{:id 1} {:id 2}])
    | proves :mergesANumberWhateverItsDigits
        |~~ A number is one whatever its digits. ~~|
        ~([1 1.0] | toSet | count | eq 1)
    | proves :ordersByTheOneOrder
        |~~ The set's vector is in the one order. ~~|
        ~([2 1 2 3 1] | toSet | payload | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :countsTheDistinctValues
        |~~ A count after it counts the distinct values. ~~|
        ~([1 2 2] | toSet | count | eq 2)
    | proves :leavesTheTagsOfTheSubjectBehind
        |~~ The set of a vector under a tag stands under none, and
            `within` keeps the tag.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box[2 1 2] | get [~(toSet) ~(within ~(toSet))] | eq [#[1 2] ::Box#[1 2]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/toMap
  |~~ Answers the map the vector's pairs `[key value]` write, the reverse
      of `entries`, `[[:tea 4] [:cake 6]] | toMap` answering
      `{:tea 4 :cake 6}`, a set of pairs alike. Each key is named as
      `toKeyword` names it, `[[1 "gold"]] | toMap` answering
      `{:"1" "gold"}`, and of two pairs with one
      key the last holds. An element that is no pair is refused at its
      index, and so is a key of another kind than those a key files
      under.
      ::link~(::map/entries) ::link~(::string/toKeyword)
      ::link~(::map)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The map of the pairs. ~~| ::map
    | throws :ElementNotPairError
        |~~ Each element of the vector `toMap` reads is a pair
            `[key value]`; the path of its frame names the first that is
            not.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key of a pair is a value `toKeyword` names, a keyword, a
            string, a number, a boolean, null or a tag name; the path of
            its frame names the key of the pair that is another value, and
            `:expected` the kinds a key files under.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsBack
        |~~ A map turned into its pairs turns back into itself. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | entries | toMap | eq {:tea 4 :cake 6})
    | proves :readsASetOfPairs
        |~~ A set of pairs builds a map as a vector of them does. ~~|
        ~(#[[:tea 4]] | toMap | eq {:tea 4})
    | proves :namesEachKeyAsToKeywordNamesIt
        |~~ A number and a boolean name the keywords of their texts. ~~|
        ~([[1 "x"] [true 2]] | toMap | eq {:"1" "x" :true 2})
    | proves :keepsTheLastValueOfAKeyWrittenTwice
        |~~ A key written twice among the pairs holds its last value. ~~|
        ~([[:tea 4] [:tea 5]] | toMap | eq {:tea 5})
    | proves :ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfOne
        |~~ An element that holds one value is no pair, and is refused at
            its index.
        ~~|
        ~([[:tea 4] [:cake]] | toMap !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError /1])
    | proves :ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfThree
        |~~ An element that holds three values is no pair either. ~~|
        ~([[:tea 4 5]] | toMap !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError /0])
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAVectorKey
        |~~ A vector names no key, and is refused at its pair with the
            kinds a key files under.
        ~~|
        ~([[[1] 2]] | toMap !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /0/0 | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)] | eq [::vec/toMap/KeyKindNotTakenError ::vec /0/0 #[::boolean ::keyword ::null ::number ::string ::tag]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/toVec
  |~~ Answers the vector itself, so `map ~toVec` turns vectors, sets,
      quotes and docs into vectors.
      ::link~(::set/toVec)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The vector itself. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A vector answers itself. ~~|
        ~([2 1] | toVec | eq [2 1])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:vec/toPath
  |~~ Answers the path whose segments are the texts of the elements, each
      a key alone, a keyword, a string or a number, as `get` reads one: a
      map reads a segment as the key that spells it and a vector as the
      index its digits write. `[:items 0] | toPath` answers `/items/0`,
      the path `segments` takes apart, and `[:version 1.5] | toPath`
      answers `/version/"1.5"`, which reads the key `:"1.5"` of a map, as
      `get 1.5` does.
      ::link~(::path/segments) ::link~(::string/toPath)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The path of the segments. ~~| ::path
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each element is a key alone, a keyword, a string or a number,
            `[:items 0] | toPath`, a boolean, null and a tag name written by
            the keyword a map files them under, `:true`.
        ~~|
    | throws :SubjectEmptyError
        |~~ A path names one segment at least: the value itself is `it`,
            so the empty vector names no path.
        ~~|
    | proves :buildsAPath
        |~~ Keys and indices build a path. ~~|
        ~([:items 0] | toPath | print | eq "/items/0")
    | proves :readsANumberAsTheSegmentOfItsText
        |~~ A number is the segment of its text, as `get` reads it. ~~|
        ~({:"1.5" 3} | get ([1.5] | toPath) | eq 3)
    | proves :SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
        |~~ The empty vector names no path. ~~|
        ~([] | toPath !| tag | eq ::vec/toPath/SubjectEmptyError)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A boolean is no key alone, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([:items true] | toPath !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | expected) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::vec/toPath/ElementKindNotTakenError #[::keyword ::number ::string] /1])
    | impl ::builtin{};

